[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Flutter's Low-Level Graphics and Layouts: CustomPainter, Custom Layouts, and RenderBox"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Flutter provides a rich library of pre-built widgets, advanced applications often require bespoke visual designs, complex layouts, or highly optimized rendering. To achieve this, Flutter exposes its underlying rendering pipeline through three powerful APIs: CustomPainter for direct canvas drawing, Custom Layouts for custom child positioning, and RenderBox for complete control over layout, painting, and hit-testing."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. CustomPainter: Direct Canvas Drawing"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The CustomPainter class allows you to draw directly onto a Canvas during the paint phase. It is ideal for creating custom charts, progress indicators, or complex vector illustrations. To use it, you subclass CustomPainter and override two methods: 'paint' (where the drawing commands are executed) and 'shouldRepaint' (which determines if the canvas needs to be redrawn when the configuration changes)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import 'dart:math' as math;
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class ProgressRingPainter extends CustomPainter {
  final double progress;
  ProgressRingPainter({required this.progress});

  @override
  void paint(Canvas canvas, Size size) {
    final center = Offset(size.width / 2, size.height / 2);
    final radius = math.min(size.width, size.height) / 2;

    final backgroundPaint = Paint()
      ..color = Colors.grey.shade200
      ..style = PaintingStyle.stroke
      ..strokeWidth = 8.0;

    final progressPaint = Paint()
      ..color = Colors.blue
      ..style = PaintingStyle.stroke
      ..strokeCap = StrokeCap.round
      ..strokeWidth = 8.0;

    canvas.drawCircle(center, radius, backgroundPaint);

    double sweepAngle = 2 * math.pi * progress;
    canvas.drawArc(
      Rect.fromCircle(center: center, radius: radius),
      -math.pi / 2,
      sweepAngle,
      false,
      progressPaint,
    );
  }

  @override
  bool shouldRepaint(covariant ProgressRingPainter oldDelegate) {
    return oldDelegate.progress != progress;
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Custom Layouts: CustomMultiChildLayout"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When standard Row, Column, or Stack widgets cannot satisfy your layout requirements, CustomMultiChildLayout provides a way to size and position multiple children programmatically. You define a 'MultiChildLayoutDelegate' where you override 'performLayout' to measure and position each child using 'layoutChild' and 'positionChild'."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class CascadeLayoutDelegate extends MultiChildLayoutDelegate {
  @override
  void performLayout(Size size) {
    double currentY = 0.0;
    double currentX = 0.0;

    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
      final String childId = 'child_$i';
      if (hasChild(childId)) {
        // Layout child with loose constraints
        Size childSize = layoutChild(
          childId,
          BoxConstraints.loose(size),
        );

        // Position child
        positionChild(childId, Offset(currentX, currentY));

        // Increment offsets for cascade effect
        currentX += childSize.width * 0.5;
        currentY += childSize.height * 0.5;
      }
    }
  }

  @override
  bool shouldRelayout(covariant MultiChildLayoutDelegate oldDelegate) {
    return false;
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. RenderBox: Low-Level Layout and Paint Control"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"RenderBox is the base class for render objects in the 2D Cartesian coordinate system. Creating a custom RenderBox is the most powerful way to build widgets, giving you direct control over layout constraints, sizing, painting, and hit-testing. This is how Flutter's core widgets like RenderPadding and RenderFlex are implemented under the hood."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class RenderSquare extends RenderBox {
  double _sizeMultiplier = 1.0;
  set sizeMultiplier(double value) {
    if (_sizeMultiplier == value) return;
    _sizeMultiplier = value;
    markNeedsLayout();
  }

  @override
  void performLayout() {
    // Respect incoming constraints
    final desiredWidth = constraints.maxWidth * 0.5 * _sizeMultiplier;
    final desiredHeight = constraints.maxHeight * 0.5 * _sizeMultiplier;
    size = constraints.constrain(Size(desiredWidth, desiredHeight));
  }

  @override
  void paint(PaintingContext context, Offset offset) {
    final Paint paint = Paint()..color = Colors.red;
    context.canvas.drawRect(offset & size, paint);
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Performance Optimization"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use RepaintBoundary: Wrap your CustomPainter or custom RenderBox in a RepaintBoundary to isolate paint operations and prevent unnecessary repaints of the parent widget tree."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Optimize shouldRepaint: Always implement shouldRepaint and shouldRelayout correctly. Returning true unconditionally forces Flutter to rebuild and repaint on every frame, degrading performance."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Object Allocations in Paint: Never instantiate Paint, Path, or other heavy objects inside the paint() method. Instantiate them once (e.g., in the constructor or as class fields) and reuse them."}]
